---
import FeaturesLayout from '../../layouts/FeaturesLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const howToJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: 'Gate a CI build on documentation coverage',
  description:
    'Use compodocx --coverageTest to make CI fail when documentation coverage drops below your threshold.',
  totalTime: 'PT5M',
  tool: [{ '@type': 'HowToTool', name: '@cngxjs/compodocx' }],
  step: [
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      position: 1,
      name: 'Pick a starting threshold',
      text: 'Most projects start at 50% and ratchet up. Set whatever number reflects the strictness you can defend on every PR.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      position: 2,
      name: 'Run compodocx with --coverageTest',
      text: 'Add --coverageTest 80 to the docs build command in your CI pipeline. Anything below 80% documentation coverage will exit non-zero.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      position: 3,
      name: 'Optionally scope to the public API',
      text: 'Pass --publicApiOnly with your dist folder to compute coverage against the published API only, so internal helpers do not drag the percentage down.',
    },
  ],
};
---

<FeaturesLayout
  currentSlug="coverage"
  title="Documentation Coverage - CI gating in compodocx"
  description="Per-symbol compodocx documentation coverage with CI threshold gating via --coverageTest and --coverageMinimumPerFile, scoped to your documented surface."
  extraJsonLd={howToJsonLd}
>
  <p class="text-cdx-text-secondary text-lg">
    Every HTML build produces a coverage report: how much of each symbol carries a description. Set
    a threshold and the CLI exits non-zero below it, so CI can gate.
  </p>

  <h2>Gating in CI</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="npx compodocx -p tsconfig.doc.json --coverageTest 80" />
  <p>
    Anything below 80% in the example above fails the build. Without a value the threshold is 70.
    Set it based on the strictness you&rsquo;re aiming for. Most projects start at 50% and move it
    up as gaps close.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code class="cdx-code">--coverageMinimumPerFile</code> makes each file meet a threshold of its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code class="cdx-code">--coverageTestThresholdFail false</code> prints a warning instead of failing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code class="cdx-code">--coverageTestShowOnlyFailed</code> lists only the files below the threshold.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code class="cdx-code">--files src/a.ts --files src/b.ts</code> with
      <code class="cdx-code">--coverageMinimumPerFile</code> checks just those files and generates no
      docs, which suits a pre-commit hook.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What counts as documented</h2>
  <p>
    A symbol and each of its members count once, and each counts as documented when it has a
    non-empty description. Tags alone (<code class="cdx-code">@param</code>,
    <code class="cdx-code">@returns</code>) do not satisfy the description requirement. Private
    members are not counted, and symbols no entry point exports are left out of the report and the
    threshold.
  </p>

  <h2>Coverage page</h2>
  <p>
    The HTML output renders a <em>Documentation Coverage</em> page at the project root: the overall percentage
    and a list of symbols you can sort and filter by name or file, each linking to its page.
    <code class="cdx-code">--disableCoverage</code> removes it. Pass an Istanbul summary with
    <code class="cdx-code">--unitTestCoverage coverage-summary.json</code> to show test coverage alongside.
  </p>

  <h2>Public-API filtering</h2>
  <p>
    <code class="cdx-code">--publicApiOnly &lt;dist&gt;</code> restricts the docs, and with them the coverage
    figure, to the names in an API Extractor report or your built package. Internal symbols stop dragging
    the percentage down, and the report focuses on the surface consumers actually see.
  </p>
</FeaturesLayout>
